1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,699
This is MATH 239, video 3. The topic is bijections and bijective proofs.

2
00:00:07,149 --> 00:00:32,450
After this video, you can say when a function is injective, surjective or bijective. You can use two inverse functions to prove that two sets have the same size. You can write a bijective proof in the form the course expects. And you can solve practice problem P1-6, assignment questions A1-1 and A1-3, and Practice Midterm question 2.

3
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:45,764
In video 1, we counted subsets and multisets with rows of circles and crosses. Each time, we turned one kind of object into another kind. Now we make this idea exact.

4
00:00:46,214 --> 00:00:57,307
Here are the 8 subsets of 1, 2, 3, and the 8 vectors of zeros and ones of length 3. The empty set goes to the vector 0, 0, 0.

5
00:00:57,757 --> 00:01:08,723
The subset with only 1 goes to 1, 0, 0. Each element i of the subset puts a 1 in position i. Each subset gets exactly one vector.

6
00:01:09,173 --> 00:01:15,487
Every vector gets exactly one arrow. No vector gets two arrows, and no vector is missed.

7
00:01:15,937 --> 00:01:24,812
Such a perfect pairing is called a bijection. If it exists, the two sets have the same size. We do not have to count either set.

8
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:34,030
A function f from A to B sends each element a of A to one element f of a in B. So each a has exactly one arrow out.

9
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:44,869
f is surjective if every b in B is the image of at least one a. So every b has at least one arrow in. The middle picture is surjective.

10
00:01:45,319 --> 00:01:56,135
f is injective if f of a equals f of a prime only when a equals a prime. So no b has two arrows in. The left picture is injective.

11
00:01:56,585 --> 00:02:01,961
The left picture is not surjective. The circled element of B has no arrow in.

12
00:02:02,411 --> 00:02:08,022
The middle picture is not injective. The circled element of B has two arrows in.

13
00:02:08,472 --> 00:02:22,829
f is bijective if it is injective and surjective. Then every b has exactly one arrow in. The right picture is a bijection. The old names are one-to-one for injective, and onto for surjective.

14
00:02:23,279 --> 00:02:35,674
Take a bijection f from A to B. Because f is surjective, every b is the image of at least one a. Because f is injective, every b is the image of at most one a.

15
00:02:36,124 --> 00:02:47,111
So every b is the image of exactly one a. The green ones count the arrows into each b. The arrows pair off all elements of A with all elements of B.

16
00:02:47,561 --> 00:03:02,601
So A and B have the same number of elements. The notes write A, double arrow, B, when a bijection exists. The converse is also true: two finite sets of the same size always have a bijection between them.

17
00:03:03,051 --> 00:03:14,123
Proposition 1.11 gives the tool we use in every bijective proof. Take a function f from A to B, and a function g from B to A.

18
00:03:14,573 --> 00:03:20,439
Assume g undoes f. That is, g of f of a equals a, for every a in A.

19
00:03:20,889 --> 00:03:27,908
Also assume f undoes g. That is, f of g of b equals b, for every b in B.

20
00:03:28,358 --> 00:03:41,521
Then f is injective. Suppose f of a equals f of a prime. Apply g to both sides. The left side becomes a, and the right side becomes a prime. So a equals a prime.

21
00:03:41,971 --> 00:03:52,403
And f is surjective. Take any b in B. Let a be g of b. Then f of a is f of g of b, which is b. So b has an arrow in.

22
00:03:52,853 --> 00:04:10,709
So f is a bijection. The same two lines, with f and g swapped, show that g is a bijection. We call f and g mutually inverse bijections, and write g equals f inverse. The notes leave this proof as Exercise 1.4 b.

23
00:04:11,159 --> 00:04:20,161
A bijective proof that A and B have the same size has four parts. Part 1: define a function f from A to B by a clear rule.

24
00:04:20,611 --> 00:04:34,243
Part 2: show that f of a is in B, for every a in A. A rule can give an object that is not in B. So check every condition in the definition of B. Students often forget this part.

25
00:04:34,693 --> 00:04:41,221
Part 3: define the inverse function g from B to A, and check that g of b is in A.

26
00:04:41,671 --> 00:04:57,991
Part 4: show both round trips. g of f of a equals a, and f of g of b equals b. Then Proposition 1.11 says f is a bijection. You do not need separate proofs of injective and surjective.

27
00:04:58,441 --> 00:05:09,087
Conclude that A and B have the same size. Assignment question A1-3 part c, and Practice Midterm question 2, ask for these parts.

28
00:05:09,537 --> 00:05:27,457
Example 1.12 makes the first slide exact. P of n is the set of all subsets of 1 to n. 0 1 to the n is the set of all vectors of length n with entries 0 or 1. Here n is 8 and S is 2, 3, 5, 7.

29
00:05:27,907 --> 00:05:40,131
The map alpha sends a subset S to a vector. Entry i is 1 if i is in S, and 0 if i is not in S. Positions 1, 2 and 3 give 0, 1, 1.

30
00:05:40,581 --> 00:05:48,111
Continue to position 8. The result is the vector 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0.

31
00:05:48,561 --> 00:06:00,487
The inverse map reads the vector back. S of alpha is the set of positions i where the entry is 1. This gives 2, 3, 5, 7 again. Each map undoes the other.

32
00:06:00,937 --> 00:06:19,283
So by Proposition 1.11, the two sets have the same size. A vector has 2 choices in each of n positions, so there are 2 to the n vectors. So there are 2 to the n subsets. This is Theorem 1.3, now proved with a bijection.

33
00:06:19,733 --> 00:06:45,205
Example 1.13 writes the proof of Theorem 1.9 as a bijection. M of n and t is the set of multisets of size n with t types. B of n plus t minus 1 and t minus 1 is the set of t minus 1 element subsets of 1 to n plus t minus 1. Here n is 10 and t is 4, so we have 13 positions.

34
00:06:45,655 --> 00:06:57,005
Take the 3 element subset S equals 2, 7, 11. Mark these positions with crosses. Sort them: s 1 is 2, s 2 is 7, s 3 is 11.

35
00:06:57,455 --> 00:07:05,433
Add two posts: s 0 equals 0 on the left, and s 4 equals n plus t, which is 14, on the right.

36
00:07:05,883 --> 00:07:16,145
The other 10 positions get circles. The crosses cut the circles into 4 runs. The differences of neighbouring posts are 2, 5, 4 and 3.

37
00:07:16,595 --> 00:07:36,264
Subtract 1 from each difference, because the run does not include the cross at its right end. m i equals s i minus s i minus 1, minus 1. This gives 1, 4, 3, 2. Each m i is at least 0, because s i minus 1 is less than s i.

38
00:07:36,714 --> 00:07:55,359
The sum of the m i is s t minus t, which is n. Here 1 plus 4 plus 3 plus 2 equals 10. So mu equals 1, 4, 3, 2 is a multiset of size 10 with 4 types. This is part 2 of the proof: the image is in M of n and t.

39
00:07:55,809 --> 00:08:12,620
For the inverse, start with a multiset mu equals m 1 to m t. Write m 1 circles, then a cross, then m 2 circles, and so on. Cross number i sits at position s i equals m 1 plus up to m i, plus i.

40
00:08:13,070 --> 00:08:18,574
For mu equals 1, 4, 3, 2: s 1 equals 1 plus 1, which is 2.

41
00:08:19,024 --> 00:08:22,821
s 2 equals 1 plus 4, plus 2, which is 7.

42
00:08:23,271 --> 00:08:31,613
s 3 equals 1 plus 4 plus 3, plus 3, which is 11. So we get back S equals 2, 7, 11.

43
00:08:32,063 --> 00:08:48,319
In general, each s i is larger than the one before, because we add m i plus 1. The last cross is at most n plus t minus 1. So S is a t minus 1 element subset of 1 to n plus t minus 1.

44
00:08:48,769 --> 00:09:11,809
The two maps undo each other. The notes leave this check as Exercise 1.6. So the two sets have the same size, n plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1. For n equals 10 and t equals 4, this is 13 choose 3, which is 286. A computer check lists all 286 multisets.

45
00:09:12,259 --> 00:09:25,741
Here is practice problem P1-6 from Assignment 1. A and B each have 7 elements. The picture shows a function f from A to B. Is it injective, surjective, or bijective?

46
00:09:26,191 --> 00:09:27,834
Pause the video and try it.

47
00:09:28,284 --> 00:09:43,537
Look at the arrows into each element of B. b 7 has two arrows in: one from a 5 and one from a 7. So f of a 5 equals f of a 7, but a 5 is not a 7. So f is not injective.

48
00:09:43,987 --> 00:09:53,587
Now look for an element of B with no arrow in. b 3 has none. No a has f of a equal to b 3. So f is not surjective.

49
00:09:54,037 --> 00:10:09,589
So f is not bijective. This agrees with the posted answer. Note one more point. A and B have the same size, 7. Equal size says that some bijection exists. It does not say that a given function is one.

50
00:10:10,039 --> 00:10:24,823
Assignment question A1-1 asks for a bijective proof. For n at least 1, the sum of n choose k over even k equals the sum of n choose k over odd k. Pause the video and try it.

51
00:10:25,273 --> 00:10:38,244
First, find two sets that the two sides count. n choose k counts the k element subsets of 1 to n. So let A be the subsets of even size, and B the subsets of odd size.

52
00:10:38,694 --> 00:10:51,579
For n equals 3, the even sizes are 0 and 2. They give 1 plus 3, which is 4 subsets. The odd sizes are 1 and 3. They give 3 plus 1, which is also 4.

53
00:10:52,029 --> 00:11:09,672
In general, a subset has exactly one size. So A is a disjoint union of the sets of k element subsets, for even k. Its size is the left side. In the same way, the size of B is the right side. Now we need a bijection from A to B.

54
00:11:10,122 --> 00:11:16,735
The map toggles the element 1. If 1 is not in S, add it. If 1 is in S, remove it.

55
00:11:17,185 --> 00:11:24,332
Take n equals 3. The empty set does not contain 1. So f of the empty set is the set 1.

56
00:11:24,782 --> 00:11:36,025
1, 2 contains 1, so f removes it and gives 2. In the same way, 1, 3 goes to 3. 2, 3 does not contain 1, so it goes to 1, 2, 3.

57
00:11:36,475 --> 00:11:47,888
Part 2: f of S is in B. The map adds or removes exactly one element. So the size changes by 1, and an even size becomes an odd size.

58
00:11:48,338 --> 00:12:06,578
Part 3 and 4: apply the toggle twice. The first toggle adds 1 and the second removes it, or the other way. So f of f of S equals S. The same rule, from B to A, is the inverse. By Proposition 1.11, f is a bijection.

59
00:12:07,028 --> 00:12:20,895
Why do we need n at least 1? The map uses the element 1. For n equals 0, the only subset is the empty set. Then the even sum is 1 and the odd sum is 0, so the identity is false.

60
00:12:21,345 --> 00:12:29,942
Here is the solution in the form a marker expects. First, define the two sets, and say why their sizes are the two sides.

61
00:12:30,392 --> 00:12:37,091
Second, define the map. Say that it uses the element 1, which exists because n is at least 1.

62
00:12:37,541 --> 00:12:45,541
Third, show that f sends A into B. The size changes by exactly 1. The same rule sends B into A.

63
00:12:45,991 --> 00:12:59,559
Fourth, show that the maps are mutually inverse: f of f of S equals S. Cite Proposition 1.11, and conclude that A and B have the same size. That is the identity.

64
00:13:00,009 --> 00:13:21,641
Assignment question A1-3. A n is the set of 0 1 vectors of length n with exactly two 1s. B n is the set of integer pairs b 1, b 2, with 0 at most b 1, at most b 2, at most n minus 2. Find a bijection and prove it. Pause the video and try it.

65
00:13:22,091 --> 00:13:36,277
Let the two 1s be in positions i and j, with i less than j. Define f of a as i minus 1, j minus 2. For the second vector, i is 1 and j is 3. So f gives 0, 1.

66
00:13:36,727 --> 00:13:46,370
Apply the rule to all 10 vectors for n equals 5. The first four give 0 0, 0 1, 0 2 and 0 3.

67
00:13:46,820 --> 00:13:52,153
The other six give 1 1, 1 2, 1 3, 2 2, 2 3 and 3 3.

68
00:13:52,603 --> 00:14:00,539
These are exactly the 10 pairs of B 5, each one time. A computer check confirms this for n up to 9.

69
00:14:00,989 --> 00:14:09,352
Now part c, the proof. Here is a vector of length 7 with its 1s at positions i equals 2 and j equals 5.

70
00:14:09,802 --> 00:14:31,626
Here f of a is 2 minus 1, 5 minus 2, which is 1, 3. In general, part 2 shows that f of a is in B n. The positions satisfy 1 at most i, i less than j, and j at most n. Because i and j are integers, i less than j means i is at most j minus 1.

71
00:14:32,076 --> 00:14:44,641
Subtract 1 and 2. So 0 is at most i minus 1, which is at most j minus 2, which is at most n minus 2. These are exactly the conditions of B n.

72
00:14:45,091 --> 00:15:02,691
Part 3: the inverse g puts 1s at positions b 1 plus 1 and b 2 plus 2. Because b 1 is at most b 2, b 1 plus 1 is less than b 2 plus 2. Both positions are between 1 and n. So g of b is in A n.

73
00:15:03,141 --> 00:15:24,539
Part 4: g of f of a has 1s at i minus 1 plus 1, which is i, and j minus 2 plus 2, which is j. So it is a. f of g of b gives b 1 plus 1 minus 1, and b 2 plus 2 minus 2. So it is b. By Proposition 1.11, f is a bijection.

74
00:15:24,989 --> 00:15:44,509
Practice Midterm question 2. A n is the set of 3 element subsets of 1 to n. B n is the set of triples of positive integers with sum at most n. Define a bijection, show that f of S is in B n, and state the inverse. Pause the video and try it.

75
00:15:44,959 --> 00:15:52,127
Sort the subset: s 1 less than s 2 less than s 3. Here n is 9 and S is 2, 5, 6.

76
00:15:52,577 --> 00:15:58,827
Measure the gaps, starting from 0. b 1 is the gap from 0 to s 1, which is 2.

77
00:15:59,277 --> 00:16:03,139
b 2 is the gap from s 1 to s 2, which is 3.

78
00:16:03,589 --> 00:16:07,514
b 3 is the gap from s 2 to s 3, which is 1.

79
00:16:07,964 --> 00:16:22,577
The gaps add up to s 3, which is 6. And s 3 is at most n. So the triple 2, 3, 1 is in B 9. So define f of S as s 1, s 2 minus s 1, s 3 minus s 2.

80
00:16:23,027 --> 00:16:42,867
The question asks you to illustrate f on the lists A 5 and B 5. The right column keeps the order of B 5 printed in the question, so the arrows cross. First, 1 2 3 goes to 1 1 1. 1 2 4 goes to 1 1 2. 1 2 5 goes to 1 1 3.

81
00:16:43,317 --> 00:16:52,021
Next, 1 3 4 goes to 1 2 1. 1 3 5 goes to 1 2 2. 1 4 5 goes to 1 3 1.

82
00:16:52,471 --> 00:17:08,002
Last, 2 3 4 goes to 2 1 1, 2 3 5 goes to 2 1 2, 2 4 5 goes to 2 2 1, and 3 4 5 goes to 3 1 1. Every triple of B 5 gets exactly one arrow.

83
00:17:08,452 --> 00:17:30,639
Here is the written solution. Define f. Then show f of S is in B n. s 1 is at least 1. The elements are integers and s 2 is larger than s 1, so s 2 minus s 1 is at least 1. The same holds for s 3 minus s 2. The three entries add up to s 3, which is at most n.

84
00:17:31,089 --> 00:17:43,782
State the inverse. It takes partial sums: b 1, then b 1 plus b 2, then b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3. Here b is 2, 2, 1. The first partial sum is 2.

85
00:17:44,232 --> 00:17:47,581
The second partial sum is 2 plus 2, which is 4.

86
00:17:48,031 --> 00:17:57,141
The third partial sum is 5. So f inverse of 2, 2, 1 is 2, 4, 5. This matches the arrow on the last slide.

87
00:17:57,591 --> 00:18:15,916
Each partial sum is larger than the one before, because each b i is at least 1. The last one is at most n. So we get a 3 element subset of 1 to n. The question does not ask for the round trips. In a full proof, you check them as in A1-3.

88
00:18:16,366 --> 00:18:29,827
To recap. A function is injective if each b has at most one arrow in, and surjective if each b has at least one. A bijection has exactly one, so the two sets have the same size.

89
00:18:30,277 --> 00:18:53,381
Proposition 1.11 is the working tool. Define f and g, show that each lands in the right set, and check both round trips. We used it for indicator vectors, multisets, A1-1, A1-3 and Practice Midterm question 2. In the next video, we prove identities by counting one set in two ways.
